\pard\b\fs36 CHAPTER I. The Lighthouse Keeper\b0\fs24\par
\pard The lighthouse at Marrow Point had stood for ninety years, and in all that time it had never once failed to burn. Old Tobias Wren, who tended it, said that a light was a promise, and a promise was not a thing to be broken because one happened to be tired. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard Every evening he climbed the one hundred and twelve steps with a can of oil in each hand, counting them under his breath as his father had taught him. At the top the lamp waited, patient and enormous, a great cut-glass eye that looked out over the grey water. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard On the night this story begins the wind came from the north-east, and it brought with it a thin cold rain that rattled on the gallery like handfuls of thrown gravel. Tobias trimmed the wick, wound the clockwork, and settled to his watch. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard Far below, the sea heaved itself against the rocks and fell back again, over and over, with the slow patience of something that has all the time in the world. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard\b\fs36 CHAPTER II. A Stranger on the Shore\b0\fs24\par
\pard It was past midnight when Tobias saw the figure on the shingle. At first he took it for a trick of the rain, a bundle of weed or a fallen spar, but the shape moved, and lifted one arm, and he understood that someone was alive down there. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard He went down faster than he had gone in twenty years, a lantern swinging in his fist and the cold biting at his lungs. The stranger was a young woman wrapped in a sodden coat, and she was shivering so hard that she could not speak. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard He carried her to the cottage at the foot of the tower and set her by the stove. When at last she could talk, she told him that her name was Imogen Hale, that her small boat had been driven onto the rocks, and that she had been rowing toward the light for three hours. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard "I never took my eyes from it," she said. "Whenever I lost hope I looked up, and there it was." \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard Tobias said nothing, but he stood a little straighter, and went back up the stairs to his lamp with something warm lodged in his chest. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard\b\fs36 CHAPTER III. What the Tide Brought\b0\fs24\par
\pard By morning the storm had blown itself out, and the sea lay flat and silver under a washed sky. Imogen walked the beach with a borrowed pair of boots, gathering what the tide had delivered: planks, rope, a single oar, and a brass-bound chest that was far too heavy to lift. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard Together they dragged it above the high-water line. The lock had rusted through, and a few blows with a hammer broke it open. Inside, wrapped in oilcloth, lay a bundle of letters tied with blue ribbon and a small, battered compass. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard The letters were addressed to a ship's captain named Edmund Hale. Imogen read the first one in silence, and then she sat down on the chest and wept, for Edmund Hale had been her grandfather, lost at sea long before she was born. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard The compass still worked. Its needle swung, steadied, and pointed unwaveringly at the lighthouse. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard\b\fs36 CHAPTER IV. Letters in Blue Ribbon\b0\fs24\par
\pard They read the letters over the course of a week, one each evening, by the light of the kitchen lamp. They were written to a wife who waited ashore, and they were full of small things: a gull that had followed the ship for a hundred miles, a cook who sang badly, the strange green colour of the water near the equator. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard In the last letter the captain wrote of a storm that was gathering, and of a light he hoped to see before dawn. "If I do not come home," he wrote, "tell the child that I looked for the lighthouse until the very end." \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard Tobias set down the page and looked at the window, where the great beam was beginning to sweep across the dusk. He had never known who the light was for. He had thought, in his plainer moments, that it was for no one in particular. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard Now he understood that it had always been for someone: for every sailor who looked up in the dark and found it there. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard\b\fs36 CHAPTER V. The Long Way Home\b0\fs24\par
\pard When the supply boat came on Friday, Imogen went back with it to the mainland, carrying the letters and the compass in her coat. She left behind her address, written in a careful hand, and a promise to return in the spring. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard The winter was long, and the nights were longer, and Tobias kept his light burning through gales and fogs and the black stillness of frost. He found that he no longer climbed the stairs alone; he climbed them with the thought of all the unseen faces turned toward the glow. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard In April, as the first crocuses opened in the lee of the cottage wall, a small boat appeared on the horizon. It was a sturdy craft with a red sail, and in the bow stood Imogen, waving both arms above her head. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard He waved back. It seemed to him then that the light had finished one long errand and begun another. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard\b\fs36 CHAPTER VI. Marrow Point at Dusk\b0\fs24\par
\pard Imogen stayed that summer, and the summer after. She learned to trim the wick and wind the clockwork, and to read the weather in the colour of the evening sky. She had a gift for it, Tobias said, the same gift her grandfather must have had. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard In time the Trinity House inspectors came, in their polished boots and serious hats, and were surprised to find two keepers where there had been one. They inspected the brass and the glass and the logbook, and pronounced everything in excellent order. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard On the evening they left, Tobias and Imogen climbed the tower together and watched the lamp kindle. The beam swept out across the water, steady and unhurried, and far off a ship's lantern answered it. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard "A promise," said Tobias quietly, "is not a thing to be broken." \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
\pard And the light burned on. \i caf\'e9\i0  \u8212? end\par
